{
 "batches": {
  "0": {
   "title": "Operators — one sitting, teach structurally, never drill",
   "note": "The most *written* words in the Aṣṭādhyāyī are not concepts: ca appears in 847 of 3,983 sūtras. Learn these as the punctuation of a sūtra. Your own difficulty-ordering study (τ=0.89 within content vocabulary) showed the decisive curation move is function-word exclusion — same here."
  },
  "1": {
   "title": "Alphabet, sound classes, substitution",
   "note": "Everything needed to decode a sūtra's phonological metalanguage: the Śiva-sūtras, pratyāhāras, it-markers, the vowel grades and the zero/substitution vocabulary."
  },
  "2": {
   "title": "The derivation engine — highest measured scope",
   "note": "These are the terms that govern the most sūtras *without being written*. pratyaya governs 1,821 sūtras (46%); prātipadika 1,190; taddhita 1,115; aṅga 613; dhātu 610."
  },
  "3": {
   "title": "Nominal system and kāraka",
   "note": "The 1.4 kāraka section plus the categories needed to decline and parse a noun. Learn the six kārakas as one block, not six separate words."
  },
  "4": {
   "title": "Verbal system",
   "note": "The ten lakāras, the two padas, the sārvadhātuka/ārdhadhātuka split, reduplication and the derived stems. iṭ is inherited into 70 sūtras; kṛt into 498."
  },
  "5": {
   "title": "Compounds and secondary derivation",
   "note": "The samāsa taxonomy and the taddhita sense-categories. This is where DSG starts to earn its keep as a lookup tool rather than a reading list."
  },
  "6": {
   "title": "Metarules, hermeneutics and the reference shelf",
   "note": "Rule-interpretation vocabulary and the names of the works. Read once, then use as an index — these are the terms that make the commentaries readable."
  }
 },
